# A number with its measurement uncertainty. `10 ± 0.1` and
# the concise `10.5(3)` of the physical tables, where the digits in brackets
# are the uncertainty in the last places, make the same kind of value. It
# propagates through arithmetic, powers and the math functions to first
# order, treating its operands as independent, and is shown in the concise
# form with the uncertainty to two digits. `±` binds more tightly than any
# other operator, `**` included: `10 ± 0.1 ** 2` is `(10 ± 0.1) ** 2`.

sheet Literals

| Name           | Value                |
| -------------- | -------------------- |
| plus-minus     | 10 ± 0.1             |
| concise        | 10.5(3)              |
| whole          | 10(3)                |
| long digits    | 1.5(11)              |
| exact          | 10.5(0)              |
| fine-structure | 7.297_352_5643(11)e-3 |

sheet Arithmetic

| Name           | Value                           |
| -------------- | ------------------------------- |
| sum            | = (10 ± 1) + (20 ± 2)           |
| product        | = (100 ± 2) * (0.2 ± 0.01)      |
| quotient       | = (100 ± 2) / (50 ± 1)          |
| exact operand  | = (10 ± 0.1) * 2                |
| power          | = (3 ± 0.1) ** 2                |
| ± before **    | = 10 ± 0.1 ** 2                 |
| ± before *     | = 10 ± 0.1 * 2                  |
| independent    | = (10 ± 1) - (10 ± 1)           |
| larger than the value | = 0.5 ± 0.8              |
| coarse         | = 1000 ± 200                    |

# Every math function carries the uncertainty to first order: the value is
# f(x) and the uncertainty is |f'(x)| times that of x. `round`, `floor`,
# `ceil` and `sign` have no slope, so they leave none; `re` and `conj` leave
# it as it was and `im` and `arg` of a real number are exact.
sheet Functions

| Function | Value                  |
| -------- | ---------------------- |
| abs      | = abs(-3 ± 0.1)        |
| sign     | = sign(-3 ± 0.1)       |
| round    | = round(2.6 ± 0.1)     |
| floor    | = floor(2.6 ± 0.1)     |
| ceil     | = ceil(2.6 ± 0.1)      |
| sqrt     | = sqrt(4 ± 0.4)        |
| exp      | = exp(1 ± 0.1)         |
| ln       | = ln(100 ± 1)          |
| log10    | = log10(100 ± 1)       |
| log2     | = log2(8 ± 0.1)        |
| sin      | = sin(0.5 ± 0.01)      |
| cos      | = cos(0.5 ± 0.01)      |
| tan      | = tan(0.5 ± 0.01)      |
| arcsin   | = arcsin(0.5 ± 0.01)   |
| arccos   | = arccos(0.5 ± 0.01)   |
| arctan   | = arctan(0.5 ± 0.01)   |
| sinh     | = sinh(1 ± 0.1)        |
| cosh     | = cosh(1 ± 0.1)        |
| tanh     | = tanh(1 ± 0.1)        |
| radians  | = radians(180 ± 1)     |
| degrees  | = degrees(1 ± 0.1)     |
| re       | = re(3 ± 0.1)          |
| im       | = im(3 ± 0.1)          |
| conj     | = conj(3 ± 0.1)        |
| arg      | = arg(-3 ± 0.1)        |
| exact    | = sin(0.5)             |

# There are no uncertain complex numbers. Where a calculation gives a
# Complex from a scientific value, the value becomes a Float, and the app
# says `uncertain complex not supported` in its bottom bar. Each of these is
# a Complex in a column of its own sheet, so that none changes the type of the
# columns above.
sheet Degraded

| Name              | Value                   |
| ----------------- | ----------------------- |
| beside Complex    | = (10 ± 1) + 3i         |
| Complex of parts  | = Complex(1 ± 0.1, 2)   |
| made a Complex    | = (1 ± 0.1).Complex     |

sheet Lengths

Length : Scientific

| Length      |
| ----------- |
| 10 ± 0.1    |
| 11 ± 0.2    |
| 12 ± 0.1    |
| 10.5(3)     |
| 10          |

# A column's format, in brackets after its type, is a number format as for a
# Float, `f` for places, `e` for an exponent, `g` for whichever reads better,
# with the sign, width and grouping flags, and `±` before it for
# value ± uncertainty: `±+10.3e-3`. `e-3` sets one exponent for the whole
# column. With no format a value is shown in the concise form, the
# uncertainty to two digits at the place it sets. The same five values in
# each column:
#   Concise       each cell sets its own places and exponent
#   PlusMinus     `±`: every value as value ± uncertainty
#   SameExponent  `e-3`: one exponent for the column
#   Aligned       `.4f`: one number of places, so the brackets stand in line
#   Both          `.3e-3`: the same exponent and places, the brackets in line
#   AllPlusMinus  `±.3e-3`: plus-minus, with the same exponent and places
# A value with no uncertainty keeps every digit it has under a format, and
# an uncertainty of `(0)`.
sheet Formats

Concise      : Scientific
PlusMinus    : Scientific[±]
SameExponent : Scientific[e-3]
Aligned      : Scientific[.4f]
Both         : Scientific[.3e-3]
AllPlusMinus : Scientific[±.3e-3]

| Concise     | PlusMinus | SameExponent | Aligned | Both | AllPlusMinus |
| ----------- | --------- | ------------ | ------- | ---- | ------------ |
| 0.0121(3)   | *         | *            | *       | *    | *            |
| 0.00873(41) | *         | *            | *       | *    | *            |
| 0.0102(2)   | *         | *            | *       | *    | *            |
| 0.0007(12)  | *         | *            | *       | *    | *            |
| 0.0155      | *         | *            | *       | *    | *            |

PlusMinus    := Concise
SameExponent := Concise
Aligned      := Concise
Both         := Concise
AllPlusMinus := Concise

# Two numbers are equal when they differ by no more than their uncertainties
# together (in quadrature): `10 ± 0.3` is the same as `10.2`, since 0.2 is
# inside 0.3, but not `11`. `<=` and `>=` hold for numbers within that
# distance, `<` and `>` only for ones clear of it. A number with no
# uncertainty counts as exact. Sorting still goes by the values alone.
sheet Comparison

| Name             | Value                      |
| ---------------- | -------------------------- |
| within sigma     | = 10 ± 0.3 == 10.2         |
| outside sigma    | = 10 ± 0.3 == 11           |
| not equal        | = 10 ± 0.3 != 10.2         |
| both uncertain   | = 10 ± 0.3 == 10.3 ± 0.1   |
| less, overlapped | = 10 ± 0.3 < 10.2          |
| at most          | = 10 ± 0.3 <= 10.2         |
| less, clear      | = 10 ± 0.3 < 11            |
| greater, clear   | = 12 ± 0.3 > 11            |
| exact operands   | = 10 == 10                 |

